the era of the Airbus a380 superjumbo  passenger jet is coming to a close  the a380 took off for the first time in  2008 and seats 500 passengers the  company had hoped that the plane would  squeeze out Boeing 747 and  revolutionised air travel passengers  have always been big fans of the a380  and have given the plane high marks for  comfort space and for the quiet ride but  airlines have been cautious about  committing to the costly double-decker  planes and in the end they just weren't  willing to pay the massive price tag to  own the flying BM of the a380 had  troubles from the start  including tensions between air buses  French and German management and  protracted production delays and cost  overruns those prompted a company  restructuring that cost thousands of  jobs after Qantas canceled eight orders  and Emirates canceled 39 the company  says it will simply put an end to  production of the a380 by 2021 let's get  a bit more on the story and for that I'm  joined in the studio by Christophe Cobra  from DW business hello to you Christophe  so you know the a380 was of course quite  a remarkable achievement at least in its  day so why wasn't it a financial success  for Airbus there are several reasons for  that Mariana one the a380 is the world's  biggest jetliner we just saw there in  the report  it has about 550 seats in a regular  configuration and selling those seats to  passengers can be a challenging second  the a380 is for engines and they consume  a lot of fuel nowadays there are smaller  jets that can also go a long distance  but they only have two engines for that  matter it consumed in much less fuel and  the fuel cost is an issue with Airlines  and the third one is the aviation market  didn't develop as airbus anticipated so  rather than the passenger numbers  increasing on the route between the  major hubs of the world New York London  Paris Hong Kong Shanghai people want to  fly basically door-to-door if I want to  fly from Hamburg to Boston I want to go  direct without going through a major hub  and for the  sort of connections the a380 is simply  too big and you can see it that no major  airline in South America has bought it  no airline in Africa has bought it all  of them are going for smaller  long-distance planes  alright so efficiency seems to be a key  factor in these developments so if they  stopped building this jumbo jet what  will be the impact on the company it is  quite a blow to Airbus and taking the  billions of euros of development into  account  Airbus didn't make a cent on the a380  overall by this decision to end the  production about three hundred three  thousand five hundred workers are  impacted however Airbus says that the  backlog and orders particularly for its  mid-range jet a320 are so big that the  jobs can be shifted within the company  all right Christopher Cobra from DW  business thanks so much  you
 

